Osteomyelitis
Infectious Disease & Sexual Health
Exquisitely localized deep bone pain, Reluctance to bear weight, Overlying non-healing ulcer (probe-to-bone), Localized marrow oedema on MRI

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IDS-032
Reason for Visit: Exquisitely localized, severe deep bone pain, high fever, and absolute inability to bear weight on the affected limb, requiring urgent assessment for deep space infection.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a patient with a rapidly worsening, excruciating pain in the lower leg accompanied by high fever and a non-healing ulcer. Urgent physical assessment is required to evaluate for acute osteomyelitis, rule out necrotizing soft tissue infection, and facilitate immediate hospital transfer prior to the next available in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My shin bone feels like it's going to explode from the inside out, and the pain is so intense I can't even put a toe on the ground."
History of Present Illness: Illness commenced insidiously over the last 2 weeks but became abruptly worse 24 hours ago, characterized by exquisitely localized, deep, and severe bone pain in the right tibia. The patient reports a high fever, severe shaking chills, and generalized systemic malaise. There is a complete reluctance and absolute inability to bear weight on the strictly affected right limb. The spouse notes a chronic, unremitting, non-healing skin ulcer overlying the anterior tibial prominence that developed following minor trauma a month ago. The pain is severe enough to cause limited active and passive range of motion in the adjacent knee and ankle joints due to secondary muscle spasm.
Functional Status: Bedbound, completely non-weight-bearing on the right leg.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living with spouse; patient is highly distressed and unable to mobilize to the bathroom.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/70 | HR: 112 | Temp: 39.2 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 85 Kg
General: Toxic appearance, diaphoretic, wincing in agony if the bed is inadvertently bumped.
Physical Exam: Right lower extremity reveals distinct localized erythema, pronounced severe swelling, and intense warmth specifically over the mid-anterior tibia. A 2cm chronic ulcer is present over the tibial crest. Using a sterile swab stick, the clinician is able to directly physically probe through the chronic ulcer entirely down to the hard bone (positive probe-to-bone test). Pulses are palpable; no crepitus to suggest gas gangrene. Clinical presentation aligns with the diagnostic tetrad: Exquisitely localized deep bone pain, Reluctance to bear weight, Overlying non-healing ulcer (probe-to-bone), and Localized marrow oedema on MRI (pending urgent hospital imaging).
Environmental Observations: Mobility aids (crutches) from a previous injury are present but patient is in too much pain to use them safely.
Medication Reconciliation: Taking regular paracetamol and ibuprofen with zero relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Osteomyelitis (secondary to contiguous spread from chronic ulcer) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Simple Cellulitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The positive probe-to-bone test, the excruciating deep quality of the pain (out of proportion to surface skin changes), and the absolute inability to bear weight.

Deep Vein Thrombosis (DVT) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of a high fever, shaking chills, a specific overlying ulcer, and the intensely localized focal point of the pain over the bone rather than the deep calf veins.

Septic Arthritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The primary site of maximal tenderness and swelling is over the mid-shaft of the tibia, not localized strictly within the joint capsule of the knee or ankle.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High (Impending systemic sepsis and permanent bone destruction)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with the classic triad of acute osteomyelitis: severe localized bone pain, high fever, and a positive probe-to-bone test indicating direct contiguous spread from a superficial ulcer. The severe pain, systemic toxicity (tachycardia, high fever), and absolute inability to bear weight indicate a severe, deep-space bacterial infection that is threatening the viability of the limb and risking overwhelming systemic sepsis. This is a medical emergency requiring immediate intravenous antibiotics and surgical evaluation. Delaying this assessment until the next in-hours period carried a profound risk of irreversible cortical bone necrosis and septic shock, wholly satisfying the criteria for urgent after-hours intervention (MBS 585-600).

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: IMMEDIATE call to 000 for ambulance transport to the Emergency Department for surgical and orthopedic assessment. The patient requires urgent blood cultures, a deep bone biopsy for definitive organism isolation (classically Staphylococcus aureus), an MRI to visualize localized marrow oedema and cortical destruction, and prolonged intravenous antibiotic therapy. Administer potent parenteral analgesia (if available) prior to transport.
Positive Behavioral Change: By strictly elevating the affected leg above the level of the heart on pillows while awaiting the ambulance, the patient will reduce the severe dependent throbbing pressure; this action will reward them with a slight reduction in the excruciating pain, reinforcing the need to keep the limb completely immobilized.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Handover to receiving Emergency Department physician and Orthopedic team.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Ambulance is en route. If the patient becomes severely confused, if the leg suddenly turns blue/cold, or if they experience a sudden drop in consciousness, the spouse must notify the 000 dispatcher to upgrade the priority.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is highly confident based on the positive probe-to-bone test; definitive MRI and bone cultures are required.
Clinical Course: The infection has penetrated deep into the marrow of the bone. This requires a prolonged hospital stay for intravenous antibiotics and potentially surgery to clean out the dead bone tissue.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not attempt to stand on that leg under any circumstances. The bone is actively infected and structurally weakened; putting weight on it could cause a catastrophic, complex fracture.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The chronic sore on your shin allowed bacteria to travel straight down and infect the core of your shinbone. Bone infections are extremely serious because blood flow inside the bone is poor, meaning oral antibiotic pills cannot reach the infection in high enough concentrations. You need strong IV antibiotics delivered directly into your bloodstream at the hospital.
Actionable Steps: Keep the leg elevated. Do not eat or drink anything right now, as you may require emergency surgery to wash out the bone tonight.

